# ── gisik-nokma configuration contract ─────────────────────────────────────
# Every variable the server and web read. scripts/check-env-example.mjs
# (pnpm check:env, run in CI) fails if code reads a variable this file
# does not document. Copy to .env and adjust; never commit .env.
#
# Convention: each section names the plan that READS its variables, and a
# variable whose plan has not landed is documented ahead of its reader, not
# live — setting it today changes nothing, because no shipped code looks at
# it. This product does not assert what it has not checked, and an operator
# must not be told to configure something inert without being told so.
#
# LIVE TODAY (everything else in this file is documented ahead of its reader):
#   DATABASE_URL, PORT, HOST, LOG_LEVEL, BETTER_AUTH_URL, BETTER_AUTH_SECRET,
#   WEB_ORIGIN, SNAPSHOT_INTERVAL_MS, SNAPSHOT_MIN_EVENTS,
#   EMBED_WORKER_INTERVAL_MS, EMBED_WORKER_ENABLED,
#   TRUST_PROXY, RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX, RATE_LIMIT_EVENTS_MAX,
#   RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SEC, ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL, SMTP_URL, MAGIC_LINK_FROM,
#   DATABASE_CA_CERT, DEPLOY_SHA
#     — exactly apps/server/src/config.ts's zod schema. BETTER_AUTH_* arrived
#       with Plan 4 Task 2, WEB_ORIGIN with Plan 4 Task 14 (it is the host of
#       every invitation link the server mints, so it stopped being inert the
#       moment invites shipped); the first four are Plan 1's; the last nine are
#       Plan 11 Task 1's. A missing or invalid one stops the server at
#       loadConfig, naming the variable.
#
#       WHAT "LIVE" MEANS FOR PLAN 11 TASK 1'S NINE, PRECISELY — because the
#       distinction this file's convention is built on gets FINER here rather
#       than disappearing. All nine are now PARSED, defaulted and REFUSED at
#       boot: a typo in any of them stops the server, naming it, which is the
#       whole reason they moved up here. ⚠ THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SPLIT THEM:
#       "Two of them are also CONSUMED today — DATABASE_CA_CERT by createPool's
#       TLS, DEPLOY_SHA by GET /healthz. The other seven have no downstream
#       reader yet ... setting TRUST_PROXY=true today changes no behaviour."
#       Measured against the tree, ALL NINE have a downstream reader now:
#       DEPLOY_SHA by /healthz, DATABASE_CA_CERT by createPool's TLS and by the
#       two seed CLI entrypoints, TRUST_PROXY by the pool/limiter/HSTS wiring,
#       the three RATE_LIMIT_* by the limiter construction, ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL
#       by the error alerter, and SMTP_URL with MAGIC_LINK_FROM by the
#       magic-link sender selection. So the third state this text was written to
#       protect has no members left, and the simpler reading is the true one:
#       every variable here changes behaviour today.
#
#       NOTE — `pnpm check:env` CANNOT keep this list honest, and that is why
#       it is written out by name. The gate fails on a variable code READS but
#       this file does not document; it has no opinion in the other direction,
#       so a variable listed here that config.ts stopped reading would pass
#       forever. The check that can fail on that is
#       apps/server/test/config.test.ts, whose exact-shape assertion over
#       loadConfig's return fails when a field appears or disappears, and whose
#       hand-maintained key list feeds this file's own values back through the
#       schema. Anyone editing this block extends that list in the same change.
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID, GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET
#     — read directly by createAuth (apps/server/src/auth/auth.ts), not through
#       config.ts. Empty is legal: it leaves the provider registered but
#       unusable rather than failing start.
#   OBJECT_STORE, OBJECT_STORE_DIR, OBJECT_STORE_S3_REGION, OBJECT_STORE_S3_BUCKET,
#   OBJECT_STORE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID, OBJECT_STORE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY,
#   OBJECT_STORE_S3_ENDPOINT, OBJECT_STORE_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE
#     — read by apps/server/src/attachments/store-config.ts's
#       createObjectStoreFromEnv (Plan 7 Task 8), not through config.ts.
#       NOTE: the "Object storage" section below (S3_ENDPOINT, S3_REGION,
#       S3_BUCKET, S3_ACCESS_KEY, S3_SECRET_KEY, S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE) predates
#       this task and names none of these; no shipped code reads that block
#       either. Left as-is rather than reconciled here — that block belongs
#       to whichever plan wrote it, not to this task.
#   TEST_DATABASE_ADMIN_URL — read by the test-database helper.
#
# `pnpm check:env` proves every variable code READS is documented here. It does
# NOT check that the documented VALUES are valid — apps/server/test/config.test.ts
# does that, by feeding this file's own values to loadConfig.

# Core (Plan 1)
DATABASE_URL=postgres://gisik:gisik@localhost:54329/gisik_nokma
PORT=3000
HOST=127.0.0.1                       # 0.0.0.0 in containers
LOG_LEVEL=info                       # fatal|error|warn|info|debug|trace

# Snapshot writer (Plan 10 Task 5). Snapshots bound per-entity replay cost:
# every SNAPSHOT_INTERVAL_MS the server folds each ticket that has run
# SNAPSHOT_MIN_EVENTS past its latest snapshot and stores the result. Neither
# is a correctness knob — a server that never writes one answers with exactly
# the same state, only slower. Both must be positive integers; the server
# refuses to start otherwise, and a zero on either is a real hazard rather
# than a silly value (a 0 interval runs the pass on every event-loop tick).
SNAPSHOT_INTERVAL_MS=300000          # five minutes
SNAPSHOT_MIN_EVENTS=20               # events past the last snapshot before a new one

# The embedding worker (notes series, Plan 3 of 6). It keeps
# `search_note_chunks` in step with the ledger — embedding a note whose body
# changed, backfilling a database that has never embedded, and re-embedding
# everything when the model changes — and it is the reason note search has a
# `mode=meaning` at all. Neither setting is a correctness knob: a longer
# interval only makes embeddings later, and search keeps answering lexically
# while they catch up.
#
# EMBED_WORKER_ENABLED=false is the one to reach for when a box is short on
# memory: the model is 130 MB resident (measured Δ 274.8 MB on first use), and
# with the worker off the server serves the whole product except meaning
# search, which answers from the chunks that exist rather than failing.
EMBED_WORKER_INTERVAL_MS=10000       # ten seconds; must be a positive integer
EMBED_WORKER_ENABLED=true            # true|false

# Time (owner decision 2026-08-20: time is Asia/Kolkata). TZ sets the Node
# process's own zone, so logs and any runtime Date formatting agree with the
# product's screens. Read by the runtime, not by application code — so the
# check-env gate never sees it; it is documented here because an operator
# needs to know it exists. The DATABASE's zone is set separately, on the
# database itself (docker-compose sets it with `-c timezone=Asia/Kolkata`
# locally; deploy/do/README.md documents the managed-Postgres step, and
# `pnpm check:db-tz` verifies it — BOTH land with Plan 11 and do not exist
# yet, so neither is a command you can run today).
TZ=Asia/Kolkata

# Managed-Postgres TLS (Plan 11 Task 1) — LIVE AND CONSUMED. The PEM CA the
# managed database presents; createPool passes it as `ssl: { ca: … }`. A
# non-empty value that is not a PEM certificate stops the server at boot rather
# than failing as an opaque TLS error on the first query.
#
# Setting this CHANGES YOUR DATABASE_URL, and the reason is worth reading once.
# `pg` applies the parsed connection string ON TOP of the options it was given
# (pg 8.23.0, lib/connection-parameters.js:60,
# `Object.assign({}, config, parse(config.connectionString))`), so a URL
# carrying any of `ssl`, `sslmode`, `sslnegotiation`, `sslcert`, `sslkey` or
# `sslrootcert` REPLACES the caller's ssl config outright — the URL wins, not
# the caller. Managed URLs all carry one (DigitalOcean's ends `?sslmode=require`),
# so passing `ssl: { ca }` beside one used to discard the CA silently. createPool
# therefore STRIPS those six parameters from the URL when a CA is set, and the
# CA becomes the trust anchor. A `?sslmode=no-verify` or `?sslmode=disable`
# alongside a non-empty CA loses to the CA: that pair contradicts itself, and it
# is resolved in the direction that cannot downgrade a deployment. A DATABASE_URL
# that carries an ssl parameter but is not URL-form (a bare socket path, say)
# stops the server at boot naming both variables, rather than quietly connecting
# without the CA.
#
# Empty is the normal local value and means "no CA supplied": the URL is passed
# through untouched and ssl is left UNSET — which does NOT mean "the connection
# string decides". With neither side supplying one, pg falls back to
# `readSSLConfigFromEnvironment()` (same file, :85), i.e. the process
# environment's PGSSLMODE/PGSSLROOTCERT, and yields false when those are unset.
DATABASE_CA_CERT=

# Auth (Plan 4 / better-auth) — LIVE. Both required; no defaults.
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET signs every session cookie and bearer token and must be at
# least 32 characters: that is better-auth's own floor, below which it warns and
# starts anyway, so config.ts refuses instead. The value below is a placeholder
# of legal length and is NOT a secret — generate a real one with
# `openssl rand -base64 32` and rotate per runbook. The previous value here was
# `change-me`, nine characters, which made this file's own "copy to .env and
# adjust" instruction end in ConfigError.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=replace-me-with-openssl-rand-base64-32
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=                    # Google OAuth (spec §6); empty disables
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Sign in with Apple. Same "empty disables" contract as Google, and it is now
# STRUCTURAL rather than a vendor coincidence: `auth/social-providers.ts` omits
# the provider key entirely unless BOTH halves of a pair are non-empty, and
# `GET /v1/auth/methods` answers from that same object, so a half-configured
# provider renders no button instead of a button that fails.
#
# Provisioned but INERT until the owner sets values (Apple Developer portal:
# Services ID, Team ID, Key ID, and a .p8 key). App Store guideline 4.8 requires
# Sign in with Apple wherever a third-party login is offered, which is why the
# code ships ahead of the credentials.
#
# APPLE_CLIENT_ID is the SERVICES ID (e.g. in.artsourced.gisiknokma.web), which
# is Apple's web `client_id` — NOT the App ID.
APPLE_CLIENT_ID=                     # Services ID; empty disables
#
# APPLE_CLIENT_SECRET IS NOT A STATIC STRING. It is a client-secret JWT that
# EXPIRES (Apple caps it at 6 months). Confirmed from the installed
# @better-auth/core@1.7.2 rather than from documentation: `apple.ts` does no
# signing at all — it imports decodeJwt/importJWK from jose only to VERIFY
# Apple's id_token — and the shared token-endpoint helper puts this value on the
# wire verbatim as `client_secret`. Mint it with
# `node scripts/mint-apple-client-secret.mjs`, which reads the three variables
# below; rotation is an operational task (docs/runbook.md).
APPLE_CLIENT_SECRET=                 # a minted ES256 JWT, not a password; expires
#
# The native app's bundle id. Optional, OMITTED rather than empty when unset —
# and NOT the harmless knob an earlier version of this comment described.
#
# better-auth builds ONE id-token audience per provider, not one per flow:
# `options.audience ?? options.appBundleIdentifier ?? options.clientId`. A bundle
# id set on its own therefore REPLACES the Services ID as the accepted audience
# and breaks every WEB sign-in, whose id-token carries the Services ID. Setting
# this for the mobile app would have silently broken sign-in on the site.
#
# `social-providers.ts` handles that: when this is set it also passes an explicit
# `audience: [APPLE_CLIENT_ID, APPLE_APP_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER]`, so both flows
# validate. Leave it empty until the native app ships Sign in with Apple.
APPLE_APP_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER=         # e.g. in.artsourced.gisiknokma; optional
#
# READ BY THE MINTING SCRIPT ONLY — never by the server. They are the key
# material APPLE_CLIENT_SECRET is derived from, documented here because an
# operator sets them and because a secret with no recorded provenance cannot be
# rotated by whoever comes next. APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY is the .p8 file's contents,
# newlines and all; Apple lets you download it exactly once.
APPLE_TEAM_ID=                       # 10 chars, top-right of the developer account
APPLE_KEY_ID=                        # the Sign in with Apple key's id
APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY=                   # -----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY----- … (multi-line)

# Web origin + proxy trust. WEB_ORIGIN is LIVE (Plan 4 Task 14 reads it through
# config.ts): it is the host of every invitation link, so a wrong value mints
# links that go somewhere else. Since Plan 11 Task 1 it must be an http(s) URL
# WITH A HOST — `localhost:3001` used to pass, because a URL parser asks only
# for a scheme, and it minted links reading `localhost:3001/invite/<token>`
# with the single-use credential already spent on them.
#
# SINCE PLAN 11 TASK 13 IT IS ALSO AN AUTH SETTING, not only a CORS one. It is
# appended to better-auth's `trustedOrigins` (`createAuth`, `apps/server/src/
# auth/auth.ts`), which otherwise holds only `BETTER_AUTH_URL`'s origin — this
# SERVER's origin, which no browser ever sends. Get it wrong and every
# cookie-carrying POST from the web app is refused with
# `403 {"code":"INVALID_ORIGIN"}` and the words "Invalid origin" render on the
# sign-in page. Measured in the compose stack before the fix, not predicted.
# It is APPENDED, never substituted, so a same-origin deployment where this
# equals BETTER_AUTH_URL keeps working unchanged.
WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3001
# TRUST_PROXY is VALIDATED at boot from Plan 11 Task 1 (only the exact strings
# `true` and `false`; anything else stops the server) and LIVE since Task 2.
# Its consumer is precise and worth naming, because it is not where a reader
# would guess: it is Fastify's own INSTANCE option, `Fastify({ trustProxy })`
# in apps/server/src/app.ts, which changes `request.ip` and `request.protocol`
# for every route. The rate limiter is what makes it MATTER, in BOTH directions:
# left off behind App Platform's router, `request.ip` is the ROUTER for every
# request, so every client in the world shares one bucket and the limiter
# becomes a shared outage; turned on in front of an UNTRUSTED hop, a caller
# picks its own `X-Forwarded-For` and mints unlimited buckets. So it is `false`
# by default and `true` is a statement that a trusted proxy is in front.
# It reaches Fastify only when buildApp is given its `security` option, which
# the server main passes and tests do not — so turning it on here still changes
# `request.ip` under no test that does not ask for it.
TRUST_PROXY=false                    # true behind App Platform's router

# Rate limits (Plan 11) — production defaults 10 and 120; dev/CI run looser.
# VALIDATED at boot from Task 1 (positive integers; a zero max would refuse
# every request and a zero window is a division by zero) and LIVE since Task 2,
# as THREE independent fixed-window buckets keyed per client IP:
#   auth   — /api/auth/* (except preflights)      at RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX
#   events — POST /v1/events, POST /v1/sync, and
#            GET /v1/sync/bootstrap               at RATE_LIMIT_EVENTS_MAX
#   invite — POST /v1/workspaces/:id/invites      at RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX
# The invite bucket deliberately has NO variable of its own: minting an
# invitation is the same kind of scarce act as asking for a sign-in code — each
# one produces a credential that grants membership when spent — so it takes the
# auth budget rather than the far larger events one, through a separate limiter
# so the two cannot spend each other's window. WS /v1/stream is excluded by
# decision (a 429 on an upgrade is unreadable to the client; stream/routes.ts
# caps concurrent sockets per user instead).
RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX=10
RATE_LIMIT_EVENTS_MAX=120
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SEC=60

# Magic-link email (Plan 11). VALIDATED at boot from Task 1 — SMTP_URL must be
# an smtp(s) URL with a host and MAGIC_LINK_FROM an email address — and
# CONSUMED: `auth/select-senders.ts` picks the SMTP sender whenever SMTP_URL is
# set and the log sender when it is empty, which is the DEV path rather than a
# broken server. ⚠ This read "CONSUMED by the SMTP magic-link sender task.
# Until that lands the log sender is what sends"; that sender has landed.
# MAGIC_LINK_FROM's default is this .local dev
# address on purpose: the production sender belongs in deploy/do/app.yaml
# (Task 15), not in a code default.
SMTP_URL=smtp://localhost:1025
MAGIC_LINK_FROM=sign-in@gisik-nokma.local
# Invitation email (access plan W5). Optional — EMPTY means "the MAGIC_LINK_FROM
# address", which is the production answer today. Consumed by
# `invites/invite-mailer.ts` through `selectInviteMailer`: SMTP_URL set sends
# real mail, unset logs one line per invitation WITHOUT the link.
INVITE_FROM=

# Error alerting (Plan 11) — any Slack-compatible webhook; empty = no alerting.
# VALIDATED at boot from Task 1 (a non-empty value must be an http(s) URL with
# a host), CONSUMED by the alerting task. Nothing posts anywhere today.
ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# The hosted MCP door's switch (remote-MCP plan FD1) — the PUBLIC origin of this
# API, e.g. https://pm-api.artsourced.in. It is the OAuth issuer and the /mcp
# resource URL; NOT BETTER_AUTH_URL (the WEB origin in production). Empty = the
# hosted door (/mcp, /oauth/*, /.well-known/oauth-*) is not registered at all.
MCP_PUBLIC_URL=

# Deploy identity (Plan 11 Task 1) — LIVE AND CONSUMED. The commit this build
# was made from, and the ONE thing GET /healthz discloses beyond up/down
# (Ruling P33; the reasoning is recorded at the handler). It is a SHA and not
# the semver in package.json, because the semver moves on release while what an
# operator watching a rollout needs is which commit is answering. No hosting
# platform supplies this on its own: deploy/do/app.yaml (Task 15) sets it, and
# the default below is what an un-wired build honestly is.
DEPLOY_SHA=dev

# Object storage (Plan 7 attachments + Plan 11 backups; S3 protocol —
# MinIO locally, DO Spaces or S3 in production)
S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
S3_REGION=us-east-1
S3_BUCKET=gisik-attachments
S3_ACCESS_KEY=gisik
S3_SECRET_KEY=gisik-dev-secret
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true             # true for MinIO; false for Spaces/S3

# Attachment object store (Plan 7 Task 8) — LIVE. OBJECT_STORE selects the
# implementation createObjectStoreFromEnv builds: 'local' (default; the dev/
# test disk store) writes under OBJECT_STORE_DIR; 's3' talks the S3 protocol
# and requires all four OBJECT_STORE_S3_* credentials below, or
# createObjectStoreFromEnv refuses with every missing one named in a single
# ConfigError. Any other OBJECT_STORE value is refused by name, too.
# OBJECT_STORE_S3_ENDPOINT is optional (omit it against real AWS S3, where
# the SDK derives the endpoint from the region); path-style addressing
# defaults true because that is what every S3-compatible endpoint (MinIO,
# R2) needs, not because it is AWS's own default.
#
# This is a SEPARATE variable set from the "Object storage" block just
# above: that block predates this task, names none of these variables, and
# no shipped code reads it either — left alone rather than reconciled here
# (see the file header's NOTE). Whether the two should merge is still open.
#
# SETTLED by Plan 11 Task 11 (2026-09-06): the BACKUP daemon SHARES these
# five credentials and takes only its BUCKET from its own variable
# (BACKUP_S3_BUCKET, below), so a backup can never land in the attachment
# bucket. apps/server/src/ops/object-sink.ts is stricter than
# createObjectStoreFromEnv about two of them, deliberately: it REFUSES to
# boot without OBJECT_STORE_S3_ENDPOINT and OBJECT_STORE_S3_REGION, naming
# them. A backup destination has no local-disk fallback to degrade to, and
# an unset endpoint resolves to Amazon's while an unset region used to
# default to 'us-east-1' — a wrong-cloud upload that dev never sees,
# because the MinIO values below supply both. Attachments keep the endpoint
# optional (real AWS S3 derives it from the region); backups do not.
OBJECT_STORE=local
OBJECT_STORE_DIR=var/attachments
OBJECT_STORE_S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
OBJECT_STORE_S3_REGION=us-east-1
OBJECT_STORE_S3_BUCKET=gisik-attachments
OBJECT_STORE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=gisik
OBJECT_STORE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=gisik-dev-secret
OBJECT_STORE_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true             # true for MinIO; false for Spaces/S3

# Backups (Plan 11)
BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=gisik-backups
BACKUP_TIME_UTC=21:00                # nightly pg_dump; 21:00 UTC = 02:30 IST
LEDGER_EXPORT_TIME_UTC=22:00         # weekly ledger export
LEDGER_EXPORT_DOW_UTC=0              # 0 = Sunday
# Retention (trash series Plan 2, spec §5.8): after each successful scheduled
# pg_dump and ledger export, objects under pg/ and ledger/ older than this many
# days are deleted — never the newest under each prefix, whatever its age.
# Optional, default 30; anything but a positive integer is refused at startup,
# naming this variable. `--once` never prunes.
BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Ops errands, scheduled inside the SAME backup-worker daemon as the two jobs
# above (apps/server/src/ops/schedule.ts's startScheduler; see backup-daemon-
# main.ts). Orphan objects sweep on a fixed 30-day interval with no variable
# of its own (ORPHAN_OBJECT_RETENTION_MS, a code constant — see
# sweep-orphans.ts). The notification sweep's interval IS configurable:
# optional, default 3600000 (one hour). Unset or empty both mean "use the
# default" (App Platform resolves an unset binding to ''); once read, the
# value is refused rather than clamped if it is not a positive integer
# (requirePositiveIntervalMs, apps/server/src/ops/schedule.ts) — a
# non-numeric or non-positive value would otherwise spin the daemon in a
# tight re-arm loop or never fire at all.
NOTIFICATION_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS=3600000

# The auto-close sweep (backlog/closed spec §8.2, second wave 2026-09-18) runs
# on the FIFTH job of the same daemon. Same read-once, validate-on-every-call
# shape and the same empty-means-unset handling as the notification sweep
# above, for the same reasons: optional, default 3600000 (one hour), and a
# non-positive or non-numeric value is refused with THIS name rather than
# clamped, aborting the whole scheduler before any timer is armed. The sweep
# itself is a no-op for every workspace that has not turned the setting on.
AUTO_CLOSE_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS=3600000

# The permanent-delete sweep (trash series Plan 2, spec §5.1) is the SIXTH job
# of the same daemon: every workspace, project, ticket or note whose purge mark
# has fallen due (purgeDueAt, packages/domain/src/lifecycle.ts) is erased, then
# queued attachment objects are deleted. Same read-once, validate-on-every-call
# shape and the same empty-means-unset handling as the two sweeps above:
# optional, default 3600000 (one hour); a non-positive or non-numeric value is
# refused with THIS name, aborting the whole scheduler before any timer is armed.
# The sweep deletes attachment objects, so the worker needs OBJECT_STORE=s3 and
# the attachment bucket in production (deploy/do/app.yaml), not the local default.
PURGE_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS=3600000

# (worker) how often expired better-auth sessions are deleted (devices plan Task 4); optional,
# default 86400000 (daily). Same refusal shape as the sweeps above: a non-positive or non-numeric
# value is refused with THIS name, aborting the whole scheduler before any timer is armed.
SESSION_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS=86400000

# (worker) how often the Decision-type backfill runs (wtg-part2-model T7, spec DC-3); optional, default 86400000 (daily).
# The first run is min(30000, this) after boot. Same refusal shape as the sweeps above.
DECISION_BACKFILL_INTERVAL_MS=86400000

# Seeds (Plan 11) — people are looked up, never invented
SEED_OWNER_EMAIL=
SEED_ACCOUNTANT_EMAIL=

# Web client (Plan 6 Task 14)
#
# Two variables, and the two the plan text named are gone because the shipped
# shell reads neither. NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL had no reader: apps/web mounts the
# online DataSource with baseUrl '' and Next's rewrite forwards /v1/* to
# SERVER_ORIGIN, so the browser's HTTP base is the page's own origin.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WS_URL was the wrong SHAPE as well as unread: streamUrl() appends
# /v1/stream itself and rewrites http->ws, so what belongs here is an origin,
# not a socket URL. A documented variable nothing reads is the same trap as a
# proxy rule for a path nothing serves.
#
# SERVER_ORIGIN is read by apps/web/next.config.ts, at dev/build time, to point
# the /v1/* and /api/auth/* rewrites at the API server. Never sent to a browser.
SERVER_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SERVER_URL is BAKED INTO THE BUNDLE (that is what NEXT_PUBLIC_
# means) and is the ORIGIN the WebSocket is dialled at directly, because Next's
# dev server does not proxy protocol upgrades. Both are in turbo.json's
# globalEnv so a change to either invalidates the cached build rather than
# serving a bundle compiled against the old one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SERVER_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Test-only
TEST_DATABASE_ADMIN_URL=postgres://gisik:gisik@localhost:54329/postgres

# Playwright, workflow-builder smoke (apps/web/e2e/workflow-builder.spec.ts).
# ALL FOUR ARE OPT-IN AND UNSET BY DEFAULT, and that is deliberate rather than an
# omission: the spec calls test.skip() unless the first three are present, so an
# unconfigured checkout skips it loudly instead of failing. Nothing in CI sets
# them — .github/workflows/ci.yml runs check:env and `turbo run build test
# typecheck` and nothing else, and turbo.json defines no e2e task — so this suite
# is run by hand against a seeded stack. Documented here because check:env reads
# `process.env` accesses wherever they appear, including a spec CI never runs,
# and an undocumented read is an undocumented read.
#
# The spec builds every page.goto from E2E_BASE_URL directly rather than from
# Playwright's shared baseURL, so this is the origin apps/web is actually served
# at, not the API server's.
E2E_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001
# The workspace the seeded fixtures live in — a ULID, not a name.
E2E_WORKSPACE_ID=
# Playwright storageState files (see `playwright.config.ts`), each holding a
# signed-in session. The ADMIN one is REQUIRED for the suite to run at all: it
# is in the `configured` predicate the skip is built on.
E2E_ADMIN_STORAGE_STATE=
# The MEMBER one is genuinely OPTIONAL — it is absent from that predicate, so the
# suite runs without it and only the non-admin read-only assertions are skipped.
# Stated because the difference is invisible from the variable names.
E2E_MEMBER_STORAGE_STATE=
# Set to 1 (CI's e2e step does) and `apps/web/e2e-stack/passkey-registration.spec.ts` logs its WebAuthn
# setup, console, failed requests and auth responses, each stamped in seconds. Unset: silent.
GISIK_E2E_DIAG=
# Neo refresh browser verification (apps/web/e2e-stack/neo-depth.spec.ts). OPT-IN, and UNSET by
# default: unset, the screenshots go in a folder named for today's ISO date under
# gisik-nokma-lanes/neo-refresh-verify/. Set it (YYYY-MM-DD) to name that folder yourself.
NEO_VERIFY_DATE=
# Set to 1 and apps/web/e2e-stack/viewport.spec.ts WRITES non-text-contrast-roster.json from its
# measurement (run it on a clean tree and commit the file). OPT-IN, and UNSET by default: unset, the
# spec COMPARES its verdict with the committed file and fails on a difference (F-11, case 23.357).
UPDATE_ROSTER=

# ── Dev stack (docker-compose.dev.yml) ──────────────────────────────────────
# One command brings up Postgres, MinIO (+ buckets), Mailpit, the server and
# web in one gesture:
#
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.dev.yml up -d --build
#
# Postgres in that stack is on host port 54330, NOT 54329. 54329 is the
# shared TEST Postgres this workspace's five parallel lanes already run
# against (see gisik-nokma-lanes' orchestrator state) — binding the dev
# stack to it too would either fail to start or silently point the dev
# server at the test database. Moving the dev stack to 54330 means it and
# the root docker-compose.yml (still on 54329) are now CO-RUNNABLE — a
# deliberate change from the plan this file's dev-stack values used to
# assume, which pinned both stacks to the same port so only one could run
# at a time.
#
# THE PORT ALONE DOES NOT BUY THAT, and this paragraph claimed it before it
# was true. Compose names a project after its DIRECTORY, so both files
# resolved to the same project and both declare a service called `postgres`:
# bringing up either RECREATED the other's container instead of running
# beside it, and 54329 stopped answering the moment anyone took this
# paragraph's advice. `docker-compose.dev.yml` now sets
# `name: gisik-nokma-dev`, which is what makes the sentence above correct;
# a test asserts it, because the false version survived review by being a
# claim nothing checked.
#
# A DATABASE_URL against the dev stack reads
# postgres://gisik:gisik@localhost:54330/gisik_nokma; every DATABASE_URL
# documented elsewhere in this file (54329) still targets the root stack.
#
# The dev stack's server container sets its own environment directly in
# docker-compose.dev.yml (OBJECT_STORE=s3 against the bundled MinIO,
# BETTER_AUTH_*, WEB_ORIGIN, etc.) — nothing further to copy from this file
# to run it; see that file's header comment for the full list of deviations
# from Plan 11 Task 6's original text and why each was necessary.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NATIVE CLIENT BUILD-TIME VARIABLES (apps/mobile, apps/desktop)
#
# Added 2026-09-07 by the pre-launch checklist's "no secrets stored in the
# frontend" item. These were read by shipping code and documented NOWHERE while
# `check:env` reported ok — the gate scanned `apps/server/src`, `apps/web` and
# `packages` only, so it enforced its rule on one of the three shipping clients.
# Both blind spots are now closed (`scripts/check-env-example.mjs`: the two new
# roots, and a detector for Vite's `import.meta.env`, which apps/desktop uses
# instead of `process.env`).
#
# ⚠ EVERY VARIABLE IN THIS BLOCK IS INLINED INTO THE SHIPPED CLIENT BUNDLE.
# `EXPO_PUBLIC_*` and `VITE_*` are not merely readable at runtime — the bundler
# SUBSTITUTES the literal value at build time, so it is in the artifact, in
# plain text, for anyone who unpacks it. Treat the value as published the moment
# a build is made with it set. Nothing here may ever hold a server credential.

# The API origin each native client dials. A URL, safe to be public by nature.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE=http://localhost:3000
VITE_API_BASE=http://localhost:3000

# ⚠ DEVELOPMENT ONLY — A REAL BEARER TOKEN, INLINED INTO THE BUNDLE IF SET.
#
# `apps/mobile/src/app.tsx` calls this "interim session plumbing until Plan 6's
# session handling is the reference for this app". It defaults to '' and the
# app is harmless without it. What makes it worth a warning rather than a line:
# NOTHING PREVENTS A RELEASE BUILD FROM CARRYING IT. Set it in a build that
# reaches a store or a tester and you have published a working credential for
# whichever account it belongs to, in a file anyone can read, with no way to
# tell from the running app that you did.
#
# Leave both of these EMPTY for any build that is not a developer's own.
EXPO_PUBLIC_DEV_TOKEN=
EXPO_PUBLIC_DEV_WORKSPACE=

# ── The MCP server (`packages/mcp-server`) ──────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ THESE TWO ARE INVISIBLE TO `pnpm check:env` TODAY, AND THAT IS WHY THEY ARE
# WRITTEN HERE BY HAND.
#
# The gate walks `packages/` and does see `packages/mcp-server/src/config.ts`
# (measured: 4 files). No detector fires on it. D1/D2 look for `process.env.X`
# and destructuring of `process.env`; D3 — the config-key detector — applies
# ONLY in a file that mentions `process.env` at all. `loadMcpConfig` takes
# `env: NodeJS.ProcessEnv` as a PARAMETER and reads `env['GISIK_SERVER_URL']`,
# so the string `process.env` never appears and the file reads config without
# any detector noticing.
#
# That is the same class of blind spot pre-launch item 4 closed twice (roots
# that skipped two clients, then a detector that only knew `process.env` and
# missed `import.meta.env`). Recorded rather than fixed here: widening the
# detector to follow an env object through a parameter is a change to the
# gate's behaviour, and this task is a package scaffold.
# Owner: whoever next touches `scripts/check-env-example.mjs`.

# REQUIRED. The origin of the gisik-nokma API the MCP server talks to — the same
# server your web client uses. `loadMcpConfig` refuses to start without it and
# names it in the error, because an MCP server that starts and then fails every
# call is harder to diagnose than one that will not start.
GISIK_SERVER_URL=

# Optional. Where the personal access token is cached (mode 0600). Defaults to
# `~/.gisik-nokma/mcp-token`. This file holds a real credential — point it
# somewhere private, and never at a directory that syncs or gets committed.
GISIK_MCP_TOKEN_PATH=

# Re-record the viewport suite's tap-target ratchet
# (`apps/web/e2e-viewport/tap-target-baseline.json`). NOT a runtime config — it
# is read only by `apps/web/e2e-viewport/responsive.spec.ts`, and only ever set
# for one deliberate run.
#
# Why it exists at all: without it, a missing baseline entry would be RECORDED
# from the current run and asserted against nothing, so a fresh clone or a CI
# checkout with no baseline file would report full green with zero enforcement.
# Seeding is therefore an explicit act rather than a fallback. Leave it unset;
# set it to `1` for one run only when a genuinely new route needs a baseline,
# then COMMIT the file.
UPDATE_TAP_BASELINE=

# UPDATE_TYPE_SCALE_BASELINE -- the same opt-in for the label-text (type-scale)
# ratchets, in BOTH viewport suites: `apps/web/e2e-viewport/type-scale-baseline.json`
# for the signed-out routes and `apps/web/e2e-stack/type-scale-baseline.json` for
# the signed-in ones. Identical guard and identical reasoning to
# UPDATE_TAP_BASELINE above: a missing entry FAILS rather than seeding itself.
#
# One asymmetry worth knowing before you reach for it: `recordTypeScaleBaseline`
# writes only ABSENT keys, so this cannot LOWER a number. That is deliberate --
# a branch that made things worse must not be able to re-baseline over it. After
# a genuine improvement, DELETE the key (or the file) and run once with this set.
UPDATE_TYPE_SCALE_BASELINE=

# UPDATE_ACCESSIBLE_BASELINE -- the same opt-in for the ACCESSIBLE-NAME ratchets
# (cases 13.285/13.287), in BOTH viewport suites:
# `apps/web/e2e-viewport/accessible-names-baseline.json` for the signed-out
# routes and `apps/web/e2e-stack/accessible-names-baseline.json` for the
# signed-in ones. Same guard and same reasoning as the two above: a route with
# no entry is RECORDED only when this is set, never seeded silently, because a
# checkout with no baseline file would otherwise report full green with zero
# enforcement.
#
# `recordAccessibleBaseline` writes only ABSENT keys, so this cannot LOWER a
# count. Lowering one means an `aria-label`, `role`, `aria-selected` or
# `aria-describedby` that identified an element to assistive tech is GONE, which
# is the thing the owner's rule refuses -- so it needs the key DELETED from the
# file first, deliberately, in a commit that says which element and why.
#
# It went undocumented when the gate landed and `pnpm check:env` was red on main
# for it: the gate's own task list did not name check:env, so it was never run.
UPDATE_ACCESSIBLE_BASELINE=

# --- neo shadow-clip detector (smoothness G-2, case 25.320) -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `apps/web/e2e-stack/neo-shadow-clip.spec.ts`. Both are OPTIONAL, for a developer run only.
# NEO_CLIP_ORIGIN points the screens at another origin than E2E_BASE_URL -- the way to measure a worktree's CSS, which the built stack
# image on :3001 does not carry (run `next dev -p 3111` in the worktree and set this to http://localhost:3111). Sign-in still happens
# on the stack; its host-scoped cookie reaches the lane port. Empty = the stack's own origin.
NEO_CLIP_ORIGIN=
# NEO_CLIP_REPORT_DIR, when set, makes the spec write one `<viewport>-<theme>.txt` of cuts per run (an empty report is one blank
# line = zero cuts), because rtk truncates Playwright's own output.
NEO_CLIP_REPORT_DIR=

# Smoothness G-8 browser verification (apps/web/e2e-stack/sheet-header-compact.spec.ts). OPT-IN, and UNSET by
# default (the spec skips): SHEET_COMPACT_ORIGIN is the origin of the worktree's own `next dev` on a lane port
# (never the compose stack, which serves a built image), e.g. http://localhost:3197; SHEET_COMPACT_REPORT_DIR is the
# folder the measured frames and numbers are written to (default: a folder under the OS temp dir).
SHEET_COMPACT_ORIGIN=
SHEET_COMPACT_REPORT_DIR=

# Knowledge Graph stress probe (packages/knowledge-graph-browser/tests/stress.spec.ts, case 27.205). OPT-IN, UNSET by default
# (the spec skips): KG_GPU=1 launches Chromium on the real GPU instead of SwiftShader and asserts spec section 7's desktop bar
# (>= 60 fps, worst frame <= 16.7 ms). A developer run on a machine with a GPU only; CI has none.
KG_GPU=

# Workflow and Timeline graphs stress harness (packages/flow-graph-browser, cases 29.002/29.003/29.016). OPT-IN, UNSET by default:
# FG_HEADED=1 runs the harness's Playwright project in a real window (GPU compositing) instead of headless Chromium. Only a headed
# run (or a real phone) is quoted against spec section 13's pan bars; a headless number is a floor. A developer run only; CI is headless.
FG_HEADED=

# Smoothness T14 ratchet (apps/web/e2e-stack/smoothness.spec.ts, against the stack). Both UNSET by default.
# SMOOTHNESS_RECORD=1 records from five loads per route and only LOWERS: an existing row becomes the per-field minimum of the stored
# number and this run's worst, an absent key takes the run's worst, and a stack whose data (`_fixture`) differs from the stored one is
# refused. SMOOTHNESS_RECORD=raise overwrites every row and the fixture: the explicit act for new data or for accepting a worse number;
# the diff is the ratchet moving, so both are deliberate. SMOOTHNESS_SAMPLES overrides the number of cold loads per route and width
# (default 3 to check, 5 to record).
SMOOTHNESS_RECORD=
SMOOTHNESS_SAMPLES=
